  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Stable conditions cause valley fog and low clouds through\n  Friday. \n\n- A pattern shift on Saturday will bring some atmospheric river\n  remnants to the northern Rockies with higher snow levels and \n  minimal impacts expected. \n\n- Cooling early next week with lowering snow levels.\n\nFog and low stratus yet again plague valleys and climbing above \nthe inversion is not much of a solution today as high clouds have\nstreamed overhead all day. A similar setup is expected to start \nFriday as valley inversions are quite strong. If you have access \nto a website that shows weather observations around the region you\ncan clearly see the valley inversion battlegrounds near Hamilton \nin the Bitteroot Valley and Warm Springs near Butte. Most \nlocations in north-central ID have broken out and in Lemhi County\ninversions have weakened considerably since daybreak. \n\nThe high clouds are a signal of moisture rotating into the region\nand a weak cold front on Saturday will attempt to tap into that\nfeed. Light precipitation is expected, forecast to be a tenth of \nan inch or less, in Clearwater, Idaho, and Lemhi Counties ID. Snow\nlevels will remain high generally above 6000 feet. \n\nAs they say, 'the (weather) models giveth and unfortunately\nsometimes taketh away'. The system next Tuesday we have been \ntracking had been trending higher with precipitation but todays \nrun has removed most of those gains. Light precipitation remains \nin the forecast, with the best chance to receive measurable \nprecipitation south of I-90. Snow levels slowly lower from 6000 \nfeet on Saturday to between 4500 and 4000 feet Wednesday morning, \nwith a trace to 3 inches possible above 4500 feet.",
